{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-256466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-2$ in the interval $[-2,0].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=-1.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-2)^{′}=3{x}^{2}+6x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n})-({x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}+2}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=-1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+3{x}_{0}^{2}+2}{3{x}_{0}^{2}+6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-1.2)^{3}+3(-1.2)^{2}+2}{3(-1.2)^{2}+6(-1.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.864}{(-2.88)} \\\\  & ≈-0.994,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5be0fb96facda295ea4f1057e0e4210724e204fdeb1d0b41848672cdcc1c706d"} {"question" "q-256466" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256466" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.994"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{-x}+{x}^{3}-2x$ in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{-x}+{x}^{3}-2x)^{′}=-{e}^{-x}+3{x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{-{x}_{n}}+{x}_{n}^{3}-2{x}_{n}}{-{e}^{-{x}_{n}}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{-{x}_{0}}+{x}_{0}^{3}-2{x}_{0}}{-{e}^{-{x}_{0}}+3{x}_{0}^{2}-2} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{{e}^{-0.5}+{0.5}^{3}-2\\cdot 0.5}{-{e}^{-0.5}+3\\cdot {0.5}^{2}-2} \\\\  & ≈0.355,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5be0fb96facda295ea4f1057e0e4210724e204fdeb1d0b41848672cdcc1c706d"} {"question" "q-256623" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256623" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.355"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{2x}+3{x}^{2}-2$ in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{2x}+3{x}^{2})^{′}=2{e}^{2x}+6x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{2{x}_{n}}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2}{2{e}^{2{x}_{n}}+6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{2{x}_{0}}+3{x}_{0}^{2}-2}{2{e}^{2{x}_{0}}+6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{{e}^{2\\cdot 0.5}+3\\cdot {0.5}^{2}-2}{2{e}^{2\\cdot 0.5}+6\\cdot 0.5} \\\\  & ≈0.326,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5be0fb96facda295ea4f1057e0e4210724e204fdeb1d0b41848672cdcc1c706d"} {"question" "q-256484" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256484" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.326"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-255853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+x-3$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.8.$\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-255853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{2}+x-3)^{′}=2x+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{2}+{x}_{n}-3}{2{x}_{n}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(2{x}_{n}+1)-({x}_{n}^{2}+{x}_{n}-3)}{2{x}_{n}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}^{2}+3}{2{x}_{n}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{x}_{0}^{2}+3}{2{x}_{0}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.8)^{2}+3}{2(0.8)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.64}{2.6} \\\\  & =1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-255853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5be0fb96facda295ea4f1057e0e4210724e204fdeb1d0b41848672cdcc1c706d"} {"question" "q-255853" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-255853" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{x}{10}$ in the interval $[6,8].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=7.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{x}{10})}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}}{10}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{n}-\\frac{1}{10}} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{n}-{x}_{n}}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{n}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{0}-{x}_{0}}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{0}-1} \\\\  & =7-\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7)-7}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7)-1} \\\\  & ≈7.066,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5be0fb96facda295ea4f1057e0e4210724e204fdeb1d0b41848672cdcc1c706d"} {"question" "q-256498" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256498" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7.066"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2x-1$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{4}-2x-1)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}^{4}-2x-1}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(4{x}_{n}^{3}-2)-({x}_{n}^{4}-2{x}_{n}-1)}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{4}+1}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{4}+1}{4{x}_{0}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1.5)^{4}+1}{4(1.5)^{3}-2} \\\\  & =1.407\\,608…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{3{x}_{1}^{4}-1}{4{x}_{1}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1.407\\,608…)^{4}-1}{4(1.407\\,608…)^{3}-2} \\\\  & ≈1.396,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5be0fb96facda295ea4f1057e0e4210724e204fdeb1d0b41848672cdcc1c706d"} {"question" "q-256510" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256510" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.396"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}+{x}^{3}-5x$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{x}+{x}^{3}-5x)^{′}={e}^{x}+3{x}^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}_{n}^{x}+{x}_{n}^{3}-5{x}_{n}}{{e}_{n}^{x}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}({e}_{n}^{x}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5)-({e}_{n}^{x}+{x}_{n}^{3}-5{x}_{n})}{{e}_{n}^{x}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}{e}^{{x}_{n}}+2{x}_{n}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{x}_{0}{e}^{{x}_{0}}+2{x}_{0}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{0}}}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}+3{x}_{0}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1.5){e}^{1.5}+2(1.5)^{3}-{e}^{1.5}}{{e}^{1.5}+3(1.5)^{2}-5} \\\\  & =1.442\\,762….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}}+2{x}_{1}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{1}}}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}+3{x}_{1}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1.442\\,762…){e}^{1.442\\,762…}+2(1.442\\,762…)^{3}-{e}^{1.442\\,762…}}{{e}^{1.442\\,762…}+3(1.442\\,762…)^{2}-5} \\\\  & ≈1.439,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5be0fb96facda295ea4f1057e0e4210724e204fdeb1d0b41848672cdcc1c706d"} {"question" "q-256622" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256622" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.439"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-{x}^{2}-2$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.4.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{x}-{x}^{2}-2)^{′}={e}^{x}-2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}_{n}^{x}-{x}_{n}^{2}-2}{{e}_{n}^{x}-2{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}_{0}^{x}-{x}_{0}^{2}-2}{{e}_{0}^{x}-2{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =1.4-\\frac{{e}^{1.4}-{1.4}^{2}-2}{{e}^{1.4}-2\\cdot 1.4} \\\\  & =1.4-0.075\\,844… \\\\  & =1.324\\,155….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}_{1}^{x}-{x}_{1}^{2}-2}{{e}_{1}^{x}-2{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =(1.324\\,155…)-\\frac{{e}^{1.324\\,155…}-(1.324\\,155…)^{2}-2}{{e}^{1.324\\,155…}-2\\cdot (1.324\\,155…)} \\\\  & ≈1.319,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5be0fb96facda295ea4f1057e0e4210724e204fdeb1d0b41848672cdcc1c706d"} {"question" "q-256503" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256503" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.319"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-255772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-255772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-1}{(\\frac{1}{{x}_{n}})} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-({x}_{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-{x}_{n}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{n}-{x}_{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{0}-{x}_{0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{0} \\\\  & =2(1.5)-(1.5)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5) \\\\  & =2.391\\,802….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2{x}_{1}-{x}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1} \\\\  & =2(2.391\\,802…)-(2.391\\,802…)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2.391\\,802…) \\\\  & =2.697\\,840….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈2.392$ and ${x}_{2}≈2.698,$ rounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-255772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5be0fb96facda295ea4f1057e0e4210724e204fdeb1d0b41848672cdcc1c706d"} {"question" "q-255772" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-255772" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.392"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-255772" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-255772" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.698"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})-x+1$ in the interval $[1.8,2.2].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.8.$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})-x+1)^{′}=2x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})-{x}_{n}+1}{2{x}_{n}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})-{x}_{0}+1}{2{x}_{0}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})-1} \\\\  & =1.8-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({1.8}^{2})-1.8+1}{2(1.8){sec}^{2}⁡({1.8}^{2})-1} \\\\  & =2.066\\,129…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}_{1}^{2})-{x}_{1}+1}{2{x}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}_{1}^{2})-1} \\\\  & =(2.066\\,129…)-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡((2.066\\,129…)^{2})-(2.066\\,129…)+1}{2(2.066\\,129…){sec}^{2}⁡((2.066\\,129…)^{2})-1} \\\\  & =2.017\\,679….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈2.066$ and ${x}_{2}≈2.018,$ rounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5be0fb96facda295ea4f1057e0e4210724e204fdeb1d0b41848672cdcc1c706d"} {"question" "q-256554" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256554" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.066"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256554" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256554" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.018"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-x-1$ has a root in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1.5$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}-x-1)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}-1}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}-1)-({x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}-1)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.5)^{3}+1}{3(1.5)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.347….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{2{x}_{1}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{1}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.347…)^{3}+1}{3(1.347…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.325…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2{x}_{2}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{2}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.325…)^{3}+1}{3(1.325…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.324…, \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{2{x}_{3}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{3}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.324…)^{3}+1}{3(1.324…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.324….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.32$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.32$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.32,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5be0fb96facda295ea4f1057e0e4210724e204fdeb1d0b41848672cdcc1c706d"} {"question" "q-256588" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256588" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.32"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-339602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the function $f(x)=x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2$ has a root in the interval $[0,1],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=0.1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-339602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2)^{′}=-\\frac{1}{x}+1\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-2}{(-\\frac{1}{{x}_{n}}+1)} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}({x}_{n}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-2)}{{x}_{n}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}({x}_{n}-1)-{x}_{n}({x}_{n}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-2)}{{x}_{n}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}+1)}{{x}_{n}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{x}_{0}(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{0}+1)}{{x}_{0}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1\\,(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.1)+1)}{0.1-1} \\\\  & =0.144…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1}+1)}{{x}_{1}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.144…(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.144…)+1)}{0.144⋯-1} \\\\  & =0.157…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{{x}_{2}(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{2}+1)}{{x}_{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.157…(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.157…)+1)}{0.157⋯-1} \\\\  & =0.158….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈0.16$ and ${x}_{3}≈0.16$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈0.16,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3f