mula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}-6}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}+4)-({x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}-6)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{0}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.3)^{3}+6}{3(1.3)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.145…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{2{x}_{1}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{1}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.145…)^{3}+6}{3(1.145…)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.134…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2{x}_{2}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{2}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.134…)^{3}+6}{3(1.134…)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.134….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈1.13$ and ${x}_{3}≈1.13$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.13,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5be0fb96facda295ea4f1057e0e4210724e204fdeb1d0b41848672cdcc1c706d"} {"question" "q-256582" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256582" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.13"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-339531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-{e}^{x}$  has a root in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-339531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(2{x}^{3}-{e}^{x})^{′}=6{x}^{2}-{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}}{6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}})-(2{x}_{n}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{n}})}{6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}{e}^{{x}_{n}}+{e}^{{x}_{n}}}{6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{4{x}_{0}^{3}-{x}_{0}{e}^{{x}_{0}}+{e}^{{x}_{0}}}{6{x}_{0}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1)^{3}-(1){e}^{1}+{e}^{1}}{6(1)^{2}-{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =1.218\\,873….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{4{x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}}+{e}^{{x}_{1}}}{6{x}_{1}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.218\\,873…)^{3}-(1.218\\,873…){e}^{1.218\\,873…}+{e}^{1.218\\,873…}}{6(1.218\\,873…)^{2}-{e}^{1.218\\,873…}} \\\\  & =1.175\\,790…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{4{x}_{2}^{3}-{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{2}}+{e}^{{x}_{2}}}{6{x}_{2}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.175\\,790…)^{3}-(1.175\\,790…){e}^{1.175\\,790…}+{e}^{1.175\\,790…}}{6(1.175\\,790…)^{2}-{e}^{1.175\\,790…}} \\\\  & =1.173\\,749…, \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{4{x}_{3}^{3}-{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{3}}+{e}^{{x}_{3}}}{6{x}_{3}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.173\\,749…)^{3}-(1.173\\,749…){e}^{1.173\\,749…}+{e}^{1.173\\,749…}}{6(1.173\\,749…)^{2}-{e}^{1.173\\,749…}} \\\\  & =1.173\\,744…,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.174$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.174$ are the same when rounded to three decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.174,$ rounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-339531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5be0fb96facda295ea4f1057e0e4210724e204fdeb1d0b41848672cdcc1c706d"} {"question" "q-339531" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-339531" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.174"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-8x-3$ has a root in the interval $[1,3],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=2$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{4}-8x-3)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{4}-8{x}_{n}-3}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(4{x}_{n}^{3}-8)-({x}_{n}^{4}-8{x}_{n}-3)}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}_{n}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{n}^{3}-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3({x}_{0}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{0}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({2}^{4}+1)}{4({2}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{24} \\\\  & =2.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{3({x}_{1}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{1}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3((2.125)^{4}+1)}{4((2.125)^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =2.112…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{3({x}_{2}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{2}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3((2.112…)^{4}+1)}{4((2.112…)^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =2.111….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈2.11$ and ${x}_{3}≈2.11$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈2.11,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5be0fb96facda295ea4f1057e0e4210724e204fdeb1d0b41848672cdcc1c706d"} {"question" "q-256612" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "q-256612" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.11"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/state.json" "file_sha256" "f12208ec43ed173d3aa03c3faacd5c7c0fcd1fee0269d0d978a4474b32c38423"} {"question" "e-12154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=5^{2x}-20$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_0 = 1.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/example-12154.json" "file_sha256" "6dcaf0d947feca448eb996d14120222679f8c32016fa572f99ba9daa39e73e30"} {"question" "e-12154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (5^{2x}-20)' = 2\\ln (5)\\cdot 5^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{5}^{2{x}_{n}}-20}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{2{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{5}^{2{x}_{0}}-20}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{2{x}_{0}}}. \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}-20}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{2}}. \\\\  & ≈0.938,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/example-12154.json" "file_sha256" "6dcaf0d947feca448eb996d14120222679f8c32016fa572f99ba9daa39e73e30"} {"question" "e-12155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=e^x-x^3-3$ in the interval $[4,6].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_0 = 5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/example-12155.json" "file_sha256" "f1adad63b712d51d634c84f6051e041d492b945f03b0f0e8bb35fc5bbf58d10e"} {"question" "e-12155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (e^x-x^3-3)' = e^x-3x^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-{x}_{n}^{3}-3}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-3{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-{x}_{0}^{3}-3}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-3{x}_{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{{e}^{5}-{5}^{3}-3}{{e}^{5}-3(5)^{2}} \\\\  & =4.721\\,941…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{1}^{3}-3}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-3{x}_{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =(4.721\\,941…)-\\frac{{e}^{4.721\\,941…}-(4.721\\,941…)^{3}-3}{{e}^{4.721\\,941…}-3(4.721\\,941…)^{2}} \\\\  & ≈4.632,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/example-12155.json" "file_sha256" "f1adad63b712d51d634c84f6051e041d492b945f03b0f0e8bb35fc5bbf58d10e"} {"question" "e-12156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the function $f(x)=x^5-2$ has a root in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from $x_0=1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/example-12156.json" "file_sha256" "e72fa9d7b11cff4b0d83aee667092d04fdf4a3867ca79aa3b691a9bd9102b2b4"} {"question" "e-12156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (x^5-2)' = 5x^4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{5}-2}{5{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(5{x}_{n}^{4})-({x}_{n}^{5}-2)}{5{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{n}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{4{x}_{0}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1)^{5}+2}{5(1)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{4{x}_{1}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{1}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.2)^{5}+2}{5(1.2)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.152… \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{4{x}_{2}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{2}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.152…)^{5}+2}{5(1.152…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.148….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations $x_2 \\approx 1.15$ and $x_3 \\approx 1.15$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x \\approx 1.15,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055256/example-12156.json" "file_sha256" "e72fa9d7b11cff4b0d83aee667092d04fdf4a3867ca79aa3b691a9bd9102b2b4"}] "usage" {"e-12154" [] "e-12156" [] "e-12155" [] "q-255853" [] "q-255772" []} "basis" 1815 "retractions" [[17592186092609 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nSuppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x) = x^{2} + x - 3$ in the interval $[0, 2]$. Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_{0} = 0.8$.$x_{1} =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186092607 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nSuppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x) = \\ln x - 1$ in the interval $[1, 3]$. Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_{0} = 1.5$. Round your answers to three decimal places.$x_{1} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}\n$, x_{2} \\approx$\n{{field-2}}\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"finding-a-functions-root-using-the-newton-raphson-method\"></a>"] [17592186091884 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=5^{2x}-20$ in the interval $[0,2]$. Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_0 = 1$. Round your answer to three decimal places."] [17592186091884 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (5^{2x}-20)' = 2\\ln (5)\\cdot 5^{2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{n + 1} &= x_{n} - (5^{2x_{n}} - 20)/(2\\ln (5) \\cdot 5^{2x_{n}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - (5^{2x_{0}} - 20)/(2\\ln (5) \\cdot 5^{2x_{0}}). \\\\\n&= 1 - (5^{2} - 20)/(2\\ln (5) \\cdot 5^{2}). \\\\\n&\\approx 0.938\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186091885 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=e^x-x^3-3$ in the interval $[4,6]$. Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_0 = 5$. Round your answer to three decimal places."] [17592186091885 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (e^x-x^3-3)' = e^x-3x^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{n + 1} &= x_{n} - \\frac{e^{x_{n}} - x_{n}^{3} - 3}{e^{x_{n}} - 3x_{n}^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 5$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - \\frac{e^{x_{0}} - x_{0}^{3} - 3}{e^{x_{0}} - 3x_{0}^{2}} \\\\\n&= 5 - (e^{5} - 5^{3} - 3)/(e^{5} - 3(5)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 4.721941…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} - \\frac{e^{x_{1}} - x_{1}^{3} - 3}{e^{x_{1}} - 3x_{1}^{2}} \\\\\n&= (4.721941…) - (e^{4.721941…} - (4.721941…)^{3} - 3)/(e^{4.721941…} - 3(4.721941…)^{2}) \\\\\n&\\approx 4.632\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186091886 :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)=x^5-2$ has a root in the interval $[0,2]$, use the Newton-Raphson process starting from $x_0=1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places."] [17592186091886 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (x^5-2)' = 5x^4\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{n + 1} &= x_{n} - \\frac{x_{n}^{5} - 2}{5x_{n}^{4}} \\\\\n&= (x_{n}(5x_{n}^{4}) - (x_{n}^{5} - 2))/(5x_{n}^{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4x_{n}^{5} + 2}{5x_{n}^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= \\frac{4x_{0}^{5} + 2}{5x_{0}^{4}} \\\\\n&= (4(1)^{5} + 2)/(5(1)^{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{5} \\\\\n&= 1.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= \\frac{4x_{1}^{5} + 2}{5x_{1}^{4}} \\\\\n&= (4(1.2)^{5} + 2)/(5(1.2)^{4}) \\\\\n&= 1.152… \\\\\nx_{3} &= \\frac{4x_{2}^{5} + 2}{5x_{2}^{4}} \\\\\n&= (4(1.152…)^{5} + 2)/(5(1.152…)^{4}) \\\\\n&= 1.148…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations\n\n$$\nx_2 \\approx 1.15\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_3 \\approx 1.15\n$$\n\nare the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is\n\n$$\nx \\approx 1.15\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [[17592186090167 :answer-field/id] [17592186090167 :answer-field/key] [17592186090167 :answer-field/type] [17592186090168 :answer-field/id] [17592186090168 :answer-field/key] [17592186090168 :answer-field/type] [17592186090170 :answer-field/id] [17592186090170 :answer-field/key] [17592186090170 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
